# Global coding standards

Objective: code must be **production-grade, enterprise-grade, and easy to maintain for years**.

## Language & structure
- **JavaScript for all application code** (`.js` / `.jsx`); no TypeScript sources.
- Follow **clean code** principles; small, single-purpose units inside existing files
  (see `file-colocation.mdc` — do not add a file per function).
- A file must **not exceed 1000 code lines** (blank lines, `//` comments, and
  `/* */` / JSDoc do not count). If it would go over, move work into another
  existing owner — do not invent a new file (see `file-colocation.mdc`).
- **Prefer `async/await`** over callbacks/`.then` chains.
- Write **comprehensive error handling** (no empty catches; fail loud or handle deliberately).

## Naming
- Meaningful, intention-revealing names for variables and functions.
- **Max 60 characters** for any variable or function name.

## Comments & docs
- Every function has **JSDoc**: purpose, params, returns, throws.
  Write them so an AI agent can understand intent without reading the body.
  Do **not** use JSDoc as a substitute type system (`@param {Type}`) unless a specific rule asks.
- Comments explain **why / intent**, not a line-by-line narration of what the code does.

## Formatting (target; enforced by Prettier/ESLint once wired)
- **2-space** indentation; **no trailing whitespace**; **semicolons required**.
- **Single quotes** for strings in JavaScript; **max line length 100**.

## External references
- Format with **Prettier**; obey the project **ESLint** config when present.
